{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^## (Result [4-7]|Council)|''^###|fullscore|probescore'\"' docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/DESIGN-J2.md | tail -55; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/DESIGN-J2.md | sed -n '245,415p'; rg -n 'def (fullscore|probescore|goldscore|without_previous|fullcases|fullprobe)|§7.3|7.3 Own' docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py AGENTS.md\"","aggregated_output":"107:## Council rounds (Codex thread `01a1f7a7-e78a-7721-90a6-673bc85b6af6`, read-only, memories off)\n165:## Council F (`council-f-codex.md`, DISAGREE) and what follows\n249:## Council G (`council-g-codex.md`): J2a and J2b retired; the separating test (rule written before any send)\n280:## Result 4: the frozen judgement gold without the previous conclusion (v7-noprev, 51 × 3)\n290:## Result 5: the separating test (council G's (a)), 13 targets and 8 controls with 1–100 hidden pieces, 3 repeats ($0.28)\n308:  old-2 Q6 holds rung 4 on full evidence. The blind reading of all 126 answers is the registered screen (`fullscore`) and is running.\n312:## Result 6: the 432-card blind reading of the final-request probe, scored by the locked rule (`probescore`)\n324:## Result 7: the separating test's blind reading, scored by council G's screen (`fullscore`)\n   245\t  separated. Only 8 clean targets exist, with +2 moves.\n   246\t- **This bears on D198** (Alex, 2026-10-27: a judgement reads the evidence in view; \"the previous conclusion carries\" the rest). The\n   247\t  carrier is also what keeps a wrong derivation alive for a median of 8.5 judgements.\n   248\t\n   249\t## Council G (`council-g-codex.md`): J2a and J2b retired; the separating test (rule written before any send)\n   250\t- **J2a and J2b are retired as candidates.**\n   251\t  - Rule (c) missed for both; J2b regressed d3-q18.\n   252\t  - The falsifier fired for J2a.\n   253\t  - Without the previous conclusion, J2b moves 16/24 targets against v7's 14/24, with 2/24 controls each. Its check adds about 2 on\n   254\t    top of removing the anchor.\n   255\t  - The 432-card reading finishes for the record.\n   256\t- **The separating test (council G's (a)).**\n   257\t  - **Cases:** the 21 cases with 1 to 100 hidden pieces: 13 targets and 8 controls.\n   258\t  - **Requests:** each request is rebuilt by the judge's own `build_prompt` on the store as of the stored judgement. Later\n   259\t    judgements, links, sources, relations, switches and successions are shadowed by temporary tables. **48 of 48 stored requests\n   260\t    rebuild byte for byte**, then every piece in force is shown (954 pieces restored), with no hidden line.\n   261\t  - **Arms**, v7, 3 repeats each:\n   262\t    - **fullprev**: full evidence with the previous conclusion;\n   263\t    - **fullnoprev**: full evidence without it.\n   264\t  - **Comparisons:**\n   265\t    - fullnoprev against fullprev is the anchor's effect on complete evidence, the separation itself;\n   266\t    - fullprev against v7 (clipped, with the previous conclusion) is the effect of restoring evidence under the anchor;\n   267\t    - fullnoprev against v7-noprev (clipped) is the effect of restoring evidence without the anchor.\n   268\t  - **Screen, before any claim:** fullnoprev against fullprev shows **at least 5 net target repairs** that are jointly supported by\n   269\t    both blind readers in at least 2 of 3 repeats. It loses no control and adds no refusals.\n   270\t  - **Reading:** a blind deck of every repeat of every moved case and control, plus a matched unmoved sample chosen before reading. It\n   271\t    is shuffled with the stored answers and v7 re-sends, each card showing what its answer actually received.\n   272\t  - **Budget:** about $0.4 on a new $2 ledger, `phase1b.json`. Phase 1's $3 ledger is nearly spent. The total stays inside D439's\n   273\t    $40, with about $5 across the merge test and phase 1.\n   274\t- **For Alex once the confound is separated (D198 is his):**\n   275\t  1. keep what the previous conclusion carries and change only how it is presented;\n   276\t  2. replace the carried derivation with a summary of the omitted evidence;\n   277\t  3. show the full evidence.\n   278\t  Option 1 stays within D198; options 2 and 3 change it.\n   279\t\n   280\t## Result 4: the frozen judgement gold without the previous conclusion (v7-noprev, 51 × 3)\n   281\t- **Agreement 43, 45, 45 (mean 44.3) against v7's 45.0.** Strict 42, 45, 44. Refused 2 of 153.\n   282\t- **Questions that differ:**\n   283\t  - **Regressions:** d3-q17, China share (v7 3/3 agree; noprev 0/3, rung 4→5); d1-q17 (1→0); d1-q09 and d3-q09 (3→2, one repeat\n   284\t    each).\n   285\t  - **Repairs:** d2-q22, FY2028 revenue (1→3); d2-q08, H200 authorization (2→3); d3-q18, Kyber (2→3).\n   286\t- **Reading.** At the gold's cutoff (07-14) the previous conclusions are young. Removing them is not a pure gain there: the China-share\n   287\t  question loses the consistency the previous conclusion held with the H200 theme. Any design that changes the previous conclusion\n   288\t  must hold this gold question.\n   289\t\n   290\t## Result 5: the separating test (council G's (a)), 13 targets and 8 controls with 1–100 hidden pieces, 3 repeats ($0.28)\n   291\t| Condition | Targets moved off the rejected rung (at least 2 of 3) | Controls moved |\n   292\t|---|---|---|\n   293\t| Clipped evidence, with the previous conclusion (v7 re-send) | 0 / 13 | 0 / 8 |\n   294\t| Clipped, without (v7-noprev) | 9 / 13 | 1 / 8 |\n   295\t| **Full evidence, with the previous conclusion (fullprev)** | **0 / 13** | 0 / 8 |\n   296\t| **Full evidence, without (fullnoprev)** | **8 / 13** | **0 / 8** |\n   297\t\n   298\t- **Restoring the hidden evidence moves nothing while the previous conclusion is shown.** Removing the previous conclusion moves 8 of\n   299\t  13 on complete evidence, with no control moving. The carried conclusion, not the missing evidence, holds the rejected rungs.\n   300\t- **The hidden pieces matter little to the moves.** Clipped without the previous conclusion moves 9 targets, full moves 8. The one\n   301\t  control that moved when clipped (C-old-2-Q15) is stable with full evidence: that move was evidence loss.\n   302\t- **Directions (code, against the rung both readers named):**\n   303\t  - equity commitments: old-1 Q28 3→1/2, old-2 Q29 3→2, old-3 Q33 3→2;\n   304\t  - 2027 capex: old-1 Q7 and old-3 Q7, 4→3;\n   305\t  - total CPU: old-2 Q16 1→3, old-3 Q14 1→2;\n   306\t  - FY2028 gross margin: old-3 Q37 3→2.\n   307\t\n   308\t  old-2 Q6 holds rung 4 on full evidence. The blind reading of all 126 answers is the registered screen (`fullscore`) and is running.\n   309\t- Each card is built by the comparison's own card builder from the request the answer actually received. Refusals are recomputed\n   310\t  against that request (the full deck's key refusals used the clipped request and are not used).\n   311\t\n   312\t## Result 6: the 432-card blind reading of the final-request probe, scored by the locked rule (`probescore`)\n   313\t| Arm | Targets jointly supported in at least 2 of 3 (of 24) | Paired net against v7 | Controls lost (of 24) | Refused | Rule (a)(b)(d) |\n   314\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n   315\t| v7 re-send | 1 | — | 3 (new-2 Q14, new-3 Q33, old-2 Q15) | 0 | — |\n   316\t| J2a | 2 | +1 (new-3 Q26) | 2 | 0 | **does not pass** |\n   317\t| J2b | 4 | +3 (new-2 Q4, new-3 Q26, old-2 Q16) | 1 | 0 | **does not pass** |\n   318\t\n   319\t- **Plants:** 3 of 3 unsupported by both readers.\n   320\t- **v7's own re-send loses 3 controls.** A same-rung re-answer is not always re-supported, so the readers' own noise is about 3 in 24\n   321\t  controls.\n   322\t- **J2a and J2b are retired by the rule as written** ((a): a floor of 12 and a net of 8). This agrees with D682.\n   323\t\n   324\t## Result 7: the separating test's blind reading, scored by council G's screen (`fullscore`)\n   325\t| | Full evidence, with the previous conclusion | Full evidence, without it |\n   326\t|---|---|---|\n   327\t| Targets jointly supported in at least 2 of 3 (of 13) | 0 | **4**: old-1 Q7 and old-3 Q7 (2027 capex), old-2 Q16 (total CPU), old-3 Q37 (FY2028 gross margin) |\n   328\t| Controls jointly supported (of 8) | 8 | 8 |\n   329\t| Refused (recomputed against the request actually sent) | 0 | 0 |\n   330\t\n   331\t- **Screen: net repairs 4 against the 5 required, so the screen does not pass, by one.** No control lost, no refusals added. Plants 3/3\n   332\t  unsupported.\n   333\t- **Why the moved M08 answers are not confirmed.** old-1 Q28, old-2 Q29 and old-3 Q33 moved from rung 3 to rung 2. Both readers say\n   334\t  rung 1: \"$18B runs off by fiscal year-end, leaving ~$7B plus partly-funded new deals\". The move is in the readers' direction, not far\n   335\t  enough. old-3 Q14 is 1 of 3. old-2 Q6 stays at rung 4.\n   336\t- **The mechanism is confirmed by code:**\n   337\t  - with the previous conclusion, 0 of 13 move even on complete evidence;\n   338\t  - without it, 8 of 13 move, toward the readers' rung, with 0 of 8 controls moving.\n   339\t- **The fix, removing it, is not accepted.** The screen missed by one, and the frozen gold without it is 44.3 against v7's 45.0, with\n   340\t  d3-q17 regressed.\nAGENTS.md:39:  work, as one recommendation and one test (§7.3). Settle wording, placement and where a rule is written yourself, and say so in the\nAGENTS.md:428:  allows (§7.3) starts only on his explicit word (Alex, 2026-10-27: \"我不是要你看下step 1抽取到底行不行？\").\ndocs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py:129:def without_previous(user):\ndocs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py:391:def goldscore(arm):\ndocs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py:693:def probescore(arms=('v7', 'J2a', 'J2b')):\ndocs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py:952:def fullscore(arms=('v7-fullprev', 'v7-fullnoprev')):\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py | sed -n '685,744p;818,1017p'; rg -n '\"'^### 7'\"\\\\.3|first registered|instrument|recorded before' AGENTS.md; nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '2559,2565p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py | sed -n '685,744p;818,1017p'; rg -n '\"'^### 7'\"\\\\.3|first registered|instrument|recorded before' AGENTS.md; nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '2559,2565p'\"","aggregated_output":"   685\t        checks = transport.checks\n   686\t        (folder / 'checks.json').write_text(json.dumps(checks, indent=1))\n   687\t        print('replay:', sum(c['same text'] for c in checks), 'of', len(checks), 'requests byte-identical to the archived ones;',\n   688\t              sum(c['same evidence shown'] for c in checks), 'show the same evidence')\n   689\t    else:\n   690\t        print('ledger', json.dumps(ledger.read()))\n   691\t\n   692\t\n   693\tdef probescore(arms=('v7', 'J2a', 'J2b')):\n   694\t    \"\"\"$0, after `read`: the locked decision rule (DESIGN-J2.md (a), (b), (d)) per arm against v7's re-send. Every arm x case x repeat\n   695\t    must be accounted for; an invalid-rung answer counts as refused and not supported. Jointly supported = both readers' final answer\n   696\t    mark is `supported` (a split is not).\"\"\"\n   697\t    key = json.loads(PROBE_KEY.read_text())\n   698\t    marks = json.loads((OUT / 'marks-probe.json').read_text())\n   699\t    rows = json.loads((OUT / 'cases.json').read_text())\n   700\t    supported, refused = {}, {}\n   701\t    for cid, m in marks.items():\n   702\t        if m['kind'] == 'plant':\n   703\t            continue\n   704\t        k = (m['arm'], m['case'], m['repeat'])\n   705\t        supported[k] = (m['marks'] or {}).get('answer') == 'supported'\n   706\t        refused[k] = bool(m.get('refusals'))\n   707\t    for m in key['invalid']:\n   708\t        k = (m['arm'], m['case'], m['repeat'])\n   709\t        supported[k], refused[k] = False, True\n   710\t    missing = [(a, c['case'], r) for a in arms for c in rows for r in (1, 2, 3) if (a, c['case'], r) not in supported]\n   711\t    if missing:\n   712\t        raise SystemExit(f'{len(missing)} answers not accounted for, e.g. {missing[:4]}: refused')\n   713\t    holds = {a: {c['case']: sum(supported[(a, c['case'], r)] for r in (1, 2, 3)) >= 2 for c in rows} for a in arms}\n   714\t    targets = [c['case'] for c in rows if c['role'] == 'target']\n   715\t    controls = [c['case'] for c in rows if c['role'] == 'control']\n   716\t    report = {}\n   717\t    for a in arms:\n   718\t        improved = [t for t in targets if holds[a][t] and not holds['v7'][t]]\n   719\t        worsened = [t for t in targets if holds['v7'][t] and not holds[a][t]]\n   720\t        lost = [c for c in controls if not holds[a][c]]\n   721\t        report[a] = {'targets held (>=2 of 3)': sum(holds[a][t] for t in targets), 'paired improved': improved, 'paired worsened': worsened,\n   722\t                     'net': len(improved) - len(worsened), 'controls lost': lost,\n   723\t                     'refused answers': sum(refused[(a, c['case'], r)] for c in rows for r in (1, 2, 3))}\n   724\t    for a in arms:\n   725\t        r = report[a]\n   726\t        verdict = '' if a == 'v7' else ('PASSES (a)(b)(d)' if r['targets held (>=2 of 3)'] >= 12 and r['net'] >= 8\n   727\t                                         and len(r['controls lost']) <= len(report['v7']['controls lost'])\n   728\t                                         and r['refused answers'] <= report['v7']['refused answers'] else 'does not pass (a)(b)(d)')\n   729\t        print(a, json.dumps(r), verdict)\n   730\t    plants = {cid: m['marks'] for cid, m in marks.items() if m['kind'] == 'plant'}\n   731\t    print('plants (expected not supported):', json.dumps(plants))\n   732\t    (OUT / 'probescore.json').write_text(json.dumps({'report': report, 'plants': plants}, indent=1))\n   733\t\n   734\t\n   735\tdef forkcases():\n   736\t    \"\"\"$0: per target, the judgement at which the final (both readers rejected) rung was first chosen and then held to the end: the\n   737\t    request there shows the conclusion before the error (or none, at a question's first judgement), not the error carried forward.\n   738\t    Its stored request is rebuilt byte for byte under the frozen judge.\"\"\"\n   739\t    from hivemind import model\n   740\t    frozen = FROZEN_SYSTEM.read_text(encoding='utf-8')\n   741\t    out, bad = [], []\n   742\t    for case in json.loads((OUT / 'cases.json').read_text()):\n   743\t        if case['role'] != 'target':\n   744\t            continue\n   818\t    source.close()\n   819\t    triggers = target.execute(\"select name, sql from sqlite_master where type='trigger'\").fetchall()\n   820\t    for name, _ in triggers:\n   821\t        target.execute(f'drop trigger {name}')\n   822\t    later_views = [r[0] for r in target.execute('select view_id from views where created_seq>?', (boundary_seq,))]\n   823\t    for table, column in (('judgements', 'seq'), ('evidence_links', 'seq'), ('evidence_sources', 'seq'), ('same_claims', 'seq'),\n   824\t                          ('revisions', 'seq'), ('replacements', 'seq'), ('view_switches', 'after_seq'), ('successions', 'seq'),\n   825\t                          ('days_completed', 'seq'), ('evidence', 'created_seq')):\n   826\t        target.execute(f'delete from {table} where {column}>?', (boundary_seq,))\n   827\t    marks = ','.join('?' * len(later_views))\n   828\t    for table in ('views', 'rungs', 'view_keys'):\n   829\t        target.execute(f\"delete from {table} where view_id in ({marks})\", later_views)\n   830\t    for _, sql in triggers:\n   831\t        target.execute(sql)\n   832\t    target.commit()\n   833\t    target.close()\n   834\t\n   835\t\n   836\tdef window(draw, arm, ledger_path=None, first_day='2026-07-01', last_day='2026-09-20'):\n   837\t    \"\"\"The judge-only replay of a whole window (council F): a copy of an old draw holding nothing before `first_day`; each day the old\n   838\t    system's links, evidence, sources and repeat/revision relations are fed, questions are put back on the day the old system opened\n   839\t    them (with their choices), its closures and successions are restored, and the judge's own round runs from production's own queue\n   840\t    (`round.changed_views_of_day`), so each arm builds its own conclusions, citations, theme effects and queue. 'replay' answers with\n   841\t    the old archived answers at $0 and checks every request's bytes; any other arm sends, with an empty archive of its own.\"\"\"\n   842\t    mt = load('merge_test', MERGE_TEST)\n   843\t    from hivemind import config, model\n   844\t    from hivemind.views import judging, store\n   845\t    from hivemind.views import round as round_module\n   846\t    cfg = config.load(CONFIG)\n   847\t    folder = OUT / 'window' / draw / arm\n   848\t    folder.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=False)\n   849\t    old = mt.old_store(draw)\n   850\t    day_seq = dict(old.execute('select day, seq from days_completed').fetchall())\n   851\t    days = sorted(d for d in day_seq if first_day <= d <= last_day)\n   852\t    earlier = [s for d, s in day_seq.items() if d < first_day]\n   853\t    boundary = max(earlier) if earlier else 0\n   854\t    cut_copy(mt, draw, folder / 'views.sqlite', boundary)\n   855\t    conn = store.open_db(str(folder / 'views.sqlite'))\n   856\t    archive = model.Archive(str(folder / 'archive.sqlite'))\n   857\t    all_views = [r[0] for r in old.execute('select view_id from views')]\n   858\t    created = {v: old.execute('select created_seq from views where view_id=?', (v,)).fetchone()[0] for v in all_views}\n   859\t    pending = {v for v, c in created.items() if c > boundary}\n   860\t    log = []\n   861\t    feed = mt.control_feed(draw, all_views, conn, log)\n   862\t    if arm == 'replay':\n   863\t        import threading\n   864\t        replay, lock = mt.ReplayOld(draw), threading.Lock()\n   865\t\n   866\t        def transport(provider, request):\n   867\t            with lock:\n   868\t                return replay(provider, request)\n   869\t        transport.checks, transport.replay = replay.checks, replay\n   870\t    else:\n   871\t        patch_judging(arm)\n   872\t        transport, ledger = transport_for(ledger_path)\n   873\t    previous, results, days_log = boundary, [], []\n   874\t    for day in days:\n   875\t        seq = day_seq[day]\n   876\t        for view_id in sorted(v for v in pending if created[v] <= seq):\n   877\t            mt.restore_view(conn, draw, view_id)\n   878\t            pending.discard(view_id)\n   879\t        feed(day, previous, seq, pending)\n   880\t        mt.copy_rows(conn, old, 'successions', 'seq>? and seq<=?', (previous, seq))\n   881\t        previous = seq\n   882\t        conn.commit()\n   883\t        scheduled = sorted(round_module.changed_views_of_day(conn, day))\n   884\t        old_judged = {r[0] for r in old.execute('select view_id from judgements where seq=?', (seq,))}\n   885\t        if arm == 'replay':\n   886\t            transport.replay.seq = seq\n   887\t        judged = judging.round_of_judgements(archive, cfg, conn, scheduled, seq, {'input_id': f'day:{day}', 'publication_day': day},\n   888\t                                             transport=transport) if scheduled else []\n   889\t        conn.commit()\n   890\t        mt.restore_closures(conn, draw, all_views, seq)\n   891\t        conn.commit()\n   892\t        judged_set = {j['view_id'] for j in judged}\n   893\t        for j in judged:\n   894\t            results.append({'day': day, 'view': j['view_id'], 'rung': j['rung'], 'changed': j['changed']})\n   895\t        days_log.append({'day': day, 'starting queue': len(scheduled), 'judged': len(judged), 'same set as old': judged_set == old_judged,\n   896\t                         'old only': sorted(old_judged - judged_set), 'arm only': sorted(judged_set - old_judged)})\n   897\t        print(json.dumps(dict(days_log[-1], arm=arm, draw=draw)), flush=True)\n   898\t    (folder / 'results.json').write_text(json.dumps(results, indent=1))\n   899\t    (folder / 'days.json').write_text(json.dumps(days_log, indent=1))\n   900\t    if arm == 'replay':\n   901\t        checks = transport.checks\n   902\t        (folder / 'checks.json').write_text(json.dumps(checks, indent=1))\n   903\t        print('replay:', sum(c['same text'] for c in checks), 'of', len(checks), 'requests byte-identical to the archived ones;',\n   904\t              sum(c['same evidence shown'] for c in checks), 'show the same evidence;', sum(d['same set as old'] for d in days_log), 'of', len(days_log), 'days judge the same set')\n   905\t    else:\n   906\t        print('ledger', json.dumps(ledger.read()))\n   907\t\n   908\t\n   909\tdef store_as_of(views_path, judgement_id, seq):\n   910\t    \"\"\"A read-only connection showing the store as it stood when a stored judgement's request was built: later judgements, and links,\n   911\t    sources, relations and switches written after its sequence number, are shadowed by temporary tables (unqualified reads see them).\"\"\"\n   912\t    conn = readonly(views_path)\n   913\t    conn.execute('create temp table judgements as select * from main.judgements where judgement_id < ?', (judgement_id,))\n   914\t    for table, column in (('evidence_links', 'seq'), ('evidence_sources', 'seq'), ('same_claims', 'seq'), ('revisions', 'seq'),\n   915\t                          ('replacements', 'seq'), ('view_switches', 'after_seq'), ('successions', 'seq')):\n   916\t        conn.execute(f'create temp table {table} as select * from main.{table} where {column} <= ?', (seq,))\n   917\t    return conn\n   918\t\n   919\t\n   920\tdef full_requests():\n   921\t    \"\"\"$0, council G: for each case, the stored final request rebuilt by the judge's own `build_prompt` on the store as it stood\n   922\t    (must equal the stored user text byte for byte), then the same request with every piece in force shown (no hidden line).\n   923\t    Writes fullcases.json with both user texts and the hidden count; only cases with at most 100 hidden pieces are kept.\"\"\"\n   924\t    from hivemind.views import judging, store\n   925\t    rows = json.loads((OUT / 'cases.json').read_text())\n   926\t    out, mismatched = [], []\n   927\t    original_view = judging.evidence_in_view\n   928\t    for case in rows:\n   929\t        judgement, request, _, _ = stored(case)\n   930\t        views, _ = store_paths(case['draw'])\n   931\t        conn = store_as_of(views, judgement['judgement_id'], judgement['seq'])\n   932\t        judging.evidence_in_view = original_view\n   933\t        _, user = judging.build_prompt(conn, case['view_id'], 'judging-v7')\n   934\t        stored_user = request['messages'][1]['content']\n   935\t        if user != stored_user:\n   936\t            mismatched.append(case['case'])\n   937\t            continue\n   938\t        shown, hidden = original_view(conn, case['view_id'])\n   939\t        if hidden > 100:\n   940\t            continue\n   941\t        judging.evidence_in_view = lambda c, v: (store.evidence_in_force(c, v), 0)\n   942\t        _, full_user = judging.build_prompt(conn, case['view_id'], 'judging-v7')\n   943\t        judging.evidence_in_view = original_view\n   944\t        out.append(dict(case, hidden=hidden, shown=len(shown), user_clipped=stored_user, user_full=full_user))\n   945\t    (OUT / 'fullcases.json').write_text(json.dumps(out, indent=1, ensure_ascii=False))\n   946\t    print(f'{len(rows) - len(mismatched)} of {len(rows)} stored requests rebuilt byte for byte by build_prompt on the as-of store;',\n   947\t          'mismatch: ' + ', '.join(mismatched) if mismatched else '')\n   948\t    print(len(out), 'cases with at most 100 hidden pieces:', sum(c['role'] == 'target' for c in out), 'targets,',\n   949\t          sum(c['role'] == 'control' for c in out), 'controls; hidden pieces restored:', sum(c['hidden'] for c in out))\n   950\t\n   951\t\n   952\tdef fullscore(arms=('v7-fullprev', 'v7-fullnoprev')):\n   953\t    \"\"\"$0, after `readfull`: council G's screen. Every arm x case x repeat must be marked. Jointly supported = both readers' final\n   954\t    answer mark `supported` (a split is not). Refusals are recomputed against the request each answer actually received (full evidence,\n   955\t    with or without the previous conclusion), on the store as of the stored judgement. Screen: net target repairs (no-prev holds 2 of 3,\n   956\t    with-prev does not, minus the reverse) at least 5; no control lost (held with the previous conclusion, not without); no added\n   957\t    refusals.\"\"\"\n   958\t    from hivemind.views import judging\n   959\t    key = json.loads(FULL_KEY.read_text())\n   960\t    marks = json.loads((OUT / 'marks-full.json').read_text())\n   961\t    rows = {c['case']: c for c in json.loads((OUT / 'fullcases.json').read_text()) if c['hidden'] >= 1}\n   962\t    supported = {}\n   963\t    for cid, m in marks.items():\n   964\t        if m['kind'] != 'plant':\n   965\t            supported[(m['arm'], m['case'], m['repeat'])] = (m['marks'] or {}).get('answer') == 'supported'\n   966\t    missing = [(a, c, r) for a in arms for c in rows for r in (1, 2, 3) if (a, c, r) not in supported]\n   967\t    if missing:\n   968\t        raise SystemExit(f'{len(missing)} answers not marked, e.g. {missing[:4]}: refused')\n   969\t    refused = {a: 0 for a in arms}\n   970\t    for a in arms:\n   971\t        for name, case in rows.items():\n   972\t            judgement, _, _, _ = stored(case)\n   973\t            views, _ = store_paths(case['draw'])\n   974\t            text = without_previous(case['user_full']) if a.endswith('-fullnoprev') else case['user_full']\n   975\t            for r in (1, 2, 3):\n   976\t                answer = json.loads((OUT / 'probe' / a / f'{name}-{r}.json').read_text())['answer']\n   977\t                refused[a] += bool(refusals_before(views, case['view_id'], judgement['judgement_id'], answer, text))\n   978\t    holds = {a: {c: sum(supported[(a, c, r)] for r in (1, 2, 3)) >= 2 for c in rows} for a in arms}\n   979\t    prev, noprev = arms\n   980\t    targets = [c for c in rows if rows[c]['role'] == 'target']\n   981\t    controls = [c for c in rows if rows[c]['role'] == 'control']\n   982\t    repaired = [t for t in targets if holds[noprev][t] and not holds[prev][t]]\n   983\t    broken = [t for t in targets if holds[prev][t] and not holds[noprev][t]]\n   984\t    lost = [c for c in controls if holds[prev][c] and not holds[noprev][c]]\n   985\t    passes = len(repaired) - len(broken) >= 5 and not lost and refused[noprev] <= refused[prev]\n   986\t    report = {'targets held': {a: sum(holds[a][t] for t in targets) for a in arms}, 'controls held': {a: sum(holds[a][c] for c in controls) for a in arms},\n   987\t              'repaired': repaired, 'broken': broken, 'net': len(repaired) - len(broken), 'controls lost': lost, 'refused': refused,\n   988\t              'plants': {cid: m['marks'] for cid, m in marks.items() if m['kind'] == 'plant'}, 'screen passes': passes}\n   989\t    print(json.dumps(report, indent=1))\n   990\t    (OUT / 'fullscore.json').write_text(json.dumps(report, indent=1))\n   991\t\n   992\t\n   993\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   994\t    command = sys.argv[1]\n   995\t    if command == 'cases':\n   996\t        cases()\n   997\t    elif command == 'check':\n   998\t        check()\n   999\t    elif command == 'account':\n  1000\t        account(sys.argv[2])\n  1001\t    elif command == 'probe':\n  1002\t        probe(sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3], int(sys.argv[4]))\n  1003\t    elif command == 'goldcases':\n  1004\t        goldcases()\n  1005\t    elif command == 'goldprobe':\n  1006\t        goldprobe(sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3], int(sys.argv[4]))\n  1007\t    elif command == 'goldscore':\n  1008\t        goldscore(sys.argv[2])\n  1009\t    elif command == 'probescore':\n  1010\t        probescore()\n  1011\t    elif command == 'forkcases':\n  1012\t        forkcases()\n  1013\t    elif command == 'forkprobe':\n  1014\t        forkprobe(sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3], int(sys.argv[4]))\n  1015\t    elif command == 'forkscore':\n  1016\t        forkscore()\n  1017\t    elif command == 'fullrequests':\n326:### 7.3 Iterating\n  2559\t## D198 · 2026-10-27 · A judgement reads the evidence in view, not everything in force\n  2560\t\n  2561\t- Alex, 2026-10-27, on the finding that evidence in force grows without bound (v6, NVIDIA FY2027 Data Center revenue: 574 pieces after six weeks, 415 never cited by any of its 33 judgements; 40 of 316 judge requests over 30k tokens, max 67k; judging 38% of spend and rising; the run's stall came from enumerating 300+ ids): \"这是什么问题你深度思考之后改\".\n  2562\t- What the problem is (design: `docs/research/2026-10-27-evidence-in-view/design.md`): two mechanisms. (1) Extraction links wide: `role-v14.txt` says \"every statement and number is, by default, evidence linked to a shown thesis\" and also \"link only to the thesis it directly concerns; omit statements with no thesis\"; the model attaches every business mention to the broadest thesis (2.5 pieces per item on v6; a liquid-cooling note, a SuperPOD purchase, a rack R&D story). Not changed here; the prompt audit is reported to Alex as the next slice. (2) Judging-v4 reads everything in force every time (\"Rejudge from all evidence currently in force\"); an analyst keeps what his view rests on and reads what arrived.\n  2563\t- Decided: from judging-v5 (`poc-v30`) a judgement of a thesis is shown, of its evidence in force, the arrivals (pieces linked after the thesis's fifth most recent judgement, READINGS = 5; everything when it has fewer) and the basis (pieces cited by one of its last ten judgements, BASIS = 10). The rest stays in force in the ledger; the request names the count (\"[Not shown: N more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\"); the answer may cite and push only shown ids, checked against the request that was sent (`judging.evidence_shown_in`, as the theme block is). Nothing leaves the ledger; the repeat / replace / new rules, \"in force\", the rejudgement triggers, eligibility, the theme block and dependencies are unchanged. Gated on the prompt version: v4 ledgers and configurations read in force as before.\n  2564\t- Measured ($0, `census.py`, two ledgers): among judgements after a thesis's first, the rule would have hidden 1.5% (nvda-3m) and 1.1% (r5) of the citations the judge actually made (22 of 325 and 7 of 261 judgements lose one cite, mostly third-party risk mentions listed in a \"pull the other way\" sentence); shown mean 95 and 109 pieces where in force averaged 225 and 178. Tighter rules hid 16% (cited last time + new since) or kept growing (ever cited + three readings). Arm beside control (`ab.py`, $0.03): v6, v11, v22 from the same ledger state, v4 fresh against v5: the same rung and `changed` on all three, reasons consistent, request tokens 76,476 → 12,516, 53,151 → 11,204, 25,806 → 8,425. Tests: `tests/test_evidence_in_view.py` (4, red first); suite 199.\n  2565\t- Applied to the three-month run at a day boundary: 2026-08-18 finished under `poc-v29` on the serial path (the parallel runner refuses a half-done day after a code change), `poc-v30` from 2026-08-19. Prompt `judging-v5` is `judging-v4` with the first rule reworded (2,264 → 2,437 characters; the growth is the sentence that says what is not shown and who carries it).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json,pathlib; p=pathlib.Path(\\\"poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20\\\");\nfor f in (\\\"marks-full.json\\\",\\\"marks-probe.json\\\",\\\"fullcases.json\\\"):\n d=json.loads((p/f).read_text()); print(f,len(d), next(iter(d.items())) if isinstance(d,dict) else d[0]);\nfor c,m in json.loads((p/\\\"marks-full.json\\\").read_text()).items():\n if m.get(\\\"case\\\",\\\"\\\") in (\\\"T-old-1-Q28\\\",\\\"T-old-2-Q29\\\",\\\"T-old-3-Q33\\\",\\\"T-old-3-Q14\\\") and m.get(\\\"arm\\\",\\\"\\\").endswith(\\\"fullnoprev\\\"): print(c,m)' ; ls -1 poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/deck-full | tail -16; ls -1 poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/deck-probe | tail -16\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json,pathlib; p=pathlib.Path(\\\"poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20\\\");\nfor f in (\\\"marks-full.json\\\",\\\"marks-probe.json\\\",\\\"fullcases.json\\\"):\n d=json.loads((p/f).read_text()); print(f,len(d), next(iter(d.items())) if isinstance(d,dict) else d[0]);\nfor c,m in json.loads((p/\\\"marks-full.json\\\").read_text()).items():\n if m.get(\\\"case\\\",\\\"\\\") in (\\\"T-old-1-Q28\\\",\\\"T-old-2-Q29\\\",\\\"T-old-3-Q33\\\",\\\"T-old-3-Q14\\\") and m.get(\\\"arm\\\",\\\"\\\").endswith(\\\"fullnoprev\\\"): print(c,m)' ; ls -1 poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/deck-full | tail -16; ls -1 poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/deck-probe | tail -16\"","aggregated_output":"marks-full.json 129 ('C001', {'arm': 'v7-fullnoprev', 'case': 'C-old-1-Q34', 'role': 'control', 'repeat': 1, 'rung': 3, 'stored_rung': 3, 'kind': 'probe', 'refusals': [], 'marks': {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'supported'}})\nmarks-probe.json 435 ('C001', {'arm': 'J2a', 'case': 'T-old-2-Q6', 'role': 'target', 'repeat': 2, 'rung': 4, 'stored_rung': 4, 'kind': 'probe', 'changed': False, 'refusals': [], 'marks': {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'unsupported'}})\nfullcases.json 35 {'case': 'T-new-2-Q11', 'role': 'target', 'draw': 'new-2', 'label': 'Q11', 'view_id': 'v18', 'type': 'figure', 'class': 'E', 'card': 'C187', 'mark': {'split': ['insufficient', 'unsupported']}, 'hidden': 0, 'shown': 17, 'user_clipped': 'Thesis v18 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"The number and scope of U.S. states enacting new data-center construction moratoriums or material restrictions (beyond New York\\'s one-year moratorium) in calendar 2026.\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How many U.S. states other than New York enact new data-center construction moratoriums or material restrictions during calendar 2026, as verified by state legislative records, enacted statutes and official governor announcements available by January 31, 2027? Count each state once if it enacts a statewide or geographically limited construction ban or moratorium, ends data-center construction tax breaks, requires operators to fund associated power-grid infrastructure, or introduces a binding local approval requirement for construction; exclude proposals that are not enacted.\\nLadder: rung 1 below 1 count: below 1 count / rung 2 from 1 count to below 2 count: from 1 count to below 2 count / rung 3 from 2 count to below 4 count: from 2 count to below 4 count (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 from 4 count to below 7 count: from 4 count to below 7 count / rung 5 7 count or more: 7 count or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 17 items]\\n- e1180 [fact; Al Jazeera (Andy Hirschfeld); original source New York state ; from gnews:abf7551608c81d3cf4fe39b7 2026-07-27] New York became the first U.S. state to officially impose a one-year moratorium on new data-center construction.\\n    Quote: New York last week became the first US state to officially put a one-year moratorium on new data centre construction.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1255; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1181 [fact; Al Jazeera (Andy Hirschfeld); original source Various U.S. states (unnamed in aggregate) ; from gnews:abf7551608c81d3cf4fe39b7 2026-07-27] At least a dozen other U.S. states have proposed legislation similar to New York\\'s restriction on new data-center construction, though New York\\'s legislation went the furthest.\\n    Quote: At least a dozen other states have proposed similar legislation although New York’s legislation went the furthest.\\n- e1182 [party_statement; Al Jazeera (Andy Hirschfeld); original source Greg Abbott; James Talarico ; from gnews:abf7551608c81d3cf4fe39b7 2026-07-27] In Texas, Republican Governor Greg Abbott called in late June for a ban on data-center construction in rural areas, and Democratic congressman James Talarico — in a tight U.S. Senate race — unveiled a late-last-week proposal to curb data-center construction, end construction tax breaks, require operators to pay for the power grid and give local communities more say on siting.\\n    Quote: In Texas, Republican Governor Greg Abbott called in late June for a ban on data centre construction in rural areas. On the other side of the aisle, James Talarico, a state Democratic congressman who is in a tight race with Republican Ken Paxton for a US Senate seat in what has historically been a reliably conservative state, unveiled a proposal late last week to curb data centre construction. … Th\\n- e64 [rumour; Jowi Morales, Tom\\'s Hardware; original source  ; from gnews:b680fbe2c3e9228eba33a0b4 2026-07-02] Tom\\'s Hardware reported, without naming a source, that 7 out of 10 Americans say they do not want a data center in their backyard and that this resistance led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 data center projects in the first quarter of 2026.\\n    Quote: These problems have caused Americans to push back against these projects, with 7 out of 10 saying that they do not want a data center in their backyard . This resistance has led to the delay or cancellation of at least 75 projects in just the first quarter of 2026.\\n- e166 [third_party_view; Autonocion.com (article), citing Tom\\'s Hardware; original source Tom\\'s Hardware (running tally, as cited) ; from gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb 2026-07-06] According to a running tally at Tom\\'s Hardware cited by the article, more than 75 data center projects worth around $130 billion have been blocked or delayed across the US so far in 2026, and polling covered in the same reporting found 7 out of 10 Americans saying they do not want a data center built anywhere near their home.\\n    Quote: With over $130 billion in data center projects already blocked or delayed by communities fed up with power and water demands, a zero-local-water nuclear option isn\\'t just an engineering story — it\\'s a potential unlock for the entire AI buildout. … More than 75 of these projects, worth around $130 billion, have been blocked or delayed across the US so far this year, according to a running tally at \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e3438; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e840 [fact; FOX 4 News (Alex Boyer); original source Fort Worth City Council members / City of Fort Worth ; from gnews:8ba10884932cdb1bcd660bcb 2026-07-21] Earlier in July 2026, five Fort Worth City Council members signed a letter calling for a temporary moratorium on data center construction, and the Fort Worth City Council is scheduled to vote on Aug. 11, 2026 on whether to adopt a data center moratorium that would give the city a longer window to draft guidelines future data centers would have to follow. The item reports the pushback as aimed at data centers, which the city manager says is a different land use from NVIDIA\\'s new factory.\\n    Quote: Earlier in July, five Fort Worth City Council members signed a letter calling for a temporary moratorium on data center construction. … On Aug. 11, Fort Worth City Council will vote on whether to adopt the data center moratorium, which would give the city a longer window to draft guidelines that future data centers in the city would have to abide by. … Fort Worth City Manager Jay Chapa tells FOX 4\\n- e1255 [fact; PYMNTS, citing a Foley & Lardner analysis; original source New York state; Maine legislature/Governor Janet Mills; state legislatures of Minnesota, Michigan, Pennsylvania, South Carolina, New Hampshire and Virginia (compiled by Foley & Lardner) ; from gnews:1156febd573b9bcaf066f322 2026-07-27] New York placed a one-year moratorium on construction of new data centers earlier in July 2026, the first state-wide ban of its kind; Maine\\'s legislature approved a state-wide ban but Governor Janet Mills vetoed it, and Minnesota, Michigan, Pennsylvania, South Carolina, New Hampshire and Virginia are all currently considering similar legislation, according to an analysis by law firm Foley & Lardner. The analysis said that if additional states follow suit, New York\\'s decision could become the beginning of a broader regulatory trend rather than an isolated event.\\n    Quote: Earlier this month, New York placed a one-year moratorium on construction of new data centers, the first state-wide ban of its kind. Maine’s legislature had approved a state-wide ban, bit it was vetoed by Gov. Janet Mills. Minnesota, Michigan, Pennsylvania, South Carolina, New Hampshire and Virginia are all currently considering similar legislation, according to an analysis from law firm Foley & L\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1180; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e2310 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com; original source The Information ; from gnews:75707928fe6c883bcd724702 2026-08-19] The article reports, citing The Information, that data-center bans have hit more than 500 locations, presented as one of the siting/permitting risks that could block AI data-center projects and thereby the electricity-consuming infrastructure the buildout needs.\\n    Quote: So is the chance that laws and ordinances could block these projects. The Information says data center bans have hit over 500 locations.\\n- e2920 [party_statement; Benzinga, quoting Jensen Huang; original source NVIDIA Corp. (CEO Jensen Huang) 2026-08-26; from gnews:0f6c59de72ffa6b9a2036962 2026-08-26] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said in the company\\'s earnings announcement that actual demand for NVIDIA\\'s chips is far greater than 70%, but that limited memory, power and data-center capacity are holding growth to that pace for now — attributing the constraint to supply-side bottlenecks rather than demand.\\n    Quote: Huang said actual demand for Nvidia\\'s chips is far greater than 70%, but limited memory, power, and data center capacity are holding growth to that pace for now.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e2899; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e3438 [third_party_view; TechRadar Pro (Benedict Collins); original source  ; from gnews:295caa6e12f0b739d2e14ea6 2026-08-29] Public opposition to data centers has led many U.S. state representatives to impose moratoriums on data-center construction or introduce legislation making it harder to build them, and $130 billion of AI data-center projects were cancelled or delayed in Q1 of calendar 2026 because of public opposition, TechRadar Pro reported on 2026-08-29 (no source for the figure given).\\n    Quote: So much so that many state representatives are implementing moratoriums on data centers, or introducing legislation to make it harder for data centers to be built. In Q1 of this year, $130 billion worth of AI data center projects were cancelled or delayed due to public opposition.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e166; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e3439 [party_statement; TechRadar Pro (Benedict Collins); original source Greg Abbott, Governor of Texas ; from gnews:295caa6e12f0b739d2e14ea6 2026-08-29] Texas Governor Greg Abbott, who TechRadar Pro says was once an advocate for data-center development in Texas, recently criticized data-center developers over their public image, saying they \\'got the backlash they deserve\\'.\\n    Quote: Texas Gov. Greg Abbott - who was once an advocate for data center development in Texas - recently criticized data center developers for their public image, stating they “got the backlash they deserve.”\\n- e3440 [third_party_view; TechRadar Pro (Benedict Collins); original source  ; from gnews:295caa6e12f0b739d2e14ea6 2026-08-29] Data centers have become a key issue in the 2026 U.S. midterm elections, which have drawn a record $646 million in donations from corporate entities, TechRadar Pro reported on 2026-08-29.\\n    Quote: Data centers have now become a key issue of the 2026 midterm elections, which has seen a record $646 million in donations from corporate entities.\\n- e3478 [third_party_view; Jake Conley, Yahoo Finance; original source Ellen Hazen, F.L.Putnam Investment Management ; from gnews:9f03bbc66269c49a5ec8afee 2026-08-30] Ellen Hazen, chief market strategist and portfolio manager at F.L.Putnam Investment Management, said the biggest risk is whether the trillion dollars of hyperscaler capex next year can actually be spent, citing labor shortages, permitting delays, rising NIMBY opposition and component shortages; if the money cannot be spent, she expects a second-derivative slowdown.\\n    Quote: \"The biggest risk is whether or not the trillion dollars in hyperscaler capex next year can actually be spent — we have labor shortages, we have permitting delays, we have NIMBY increasing in volume, and we have component shortages,\" Hazen said. … \"If we don\\'t get the ability to spend all of that money, then the second derivative slowing,\" Hazen added. \"That\\'s one of the things that I\\'m concerned \\n- e3714 [fact; gurufocus.com; original source Texas state/grid officials and unnamed U.S. utilities ; from gnews:b7a72f34e62112b669d71e36 2026-09-01] Texas has frozen new grid connections while officials audit data-center developers, and utilities in other U.S. markets are demanding cash upfront or proof that proposed data-center sites are actually controlled, according to GuruFocus\\'s 2026-09-01 report. The item gives no number of affected projects, megawatts or a duration for the freeze.\\n    Quote: Texas has frozen new grid connections while officials audit developers. Utilities in other markets are demanding cash upfront or proof that proposed sites are actually controlled.\\n- e3715 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source gurufocus.com 2026-09-01; from gnews:b7a72f34e62112b669d71e36 2026-09-01] Electricity interconnection requests across parts of the United States now exceed 700 gigawatts, more than ten times estimated current data-center electricity consumption, per GuruFocus\\'s 2026-09-01 report. The figure is not attributed to a named grid operator, regulator or data provider.\\n    Quote: Electricity requests across parts of the United States now exceed 700 gigawattsmore than ten times estimated current data-center consumption.\\n- e4717 [party_statement; CNBC, citing a video posted on social media by an All-In Summit attendee; original source Donald Trump (President of the United States) 2026-09-14; from gnews:4c28f13be664d1332e4f8857 2026-09-14] In a phone call put on speaker at the All-In Summit in Los Angeles on Monday 2026-09-14 while NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang was on stage, U.S. President Donald Trump said opposition to AI data-center construction is \"all a hoax,\" that \"the data centers are great, and they make people wealthy, and they make states wealthy,\" and that opponents are \"playing right into the hands of a lot of people that don\\'t want to see it happen,\" adding it could be coming from China and that \"We\\'re not going to let that happen.\" Huang replied, \"We\\'re not going to let that happen, sir.\" The remarks are a presidential endorsement of data-center construction, reported from a video posted by a summit attendee; no policy action or decision was announced.\\n    Quote: With Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang speaking on stage at the All-in Summit in Los Angeles, Trump called the tech exec, and was then put on speaker.\\n\"I\\'m telling you, it\\'s all a hoax,\" Trump said, according to a video posted on social media by an attendee. \"The data centers are great, and they make people wealthy, and they make states wealthy.\" … Those opposing data center construction are \"playing right \\n- e5051 [third_party_view; qz.com (Quartz), citing Fortune; original source Data Center Watch (project-halt data); unnamed survey for the 70% figure ; from gnews:7f7770b988d0550ff0ee82c2 2026-09-18] Roughly 70% of Americans would object to a data center being built in their community, citing strain on local power resources and higher utility bills, and community opposition stalled or halted 48 data-center projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction last year alone, according to Data Center Watch data cited by Fortune.\\n    Quote: The announcement comes as AI data centers have faced mounting public and political opposition. Roughly 70% of Americans would object to a data center being built in their community , citing strain on local power resources and higher utility bills as their leading concerns. Community opposition stalled or halted 48 projects representing more than $156 billion in planned construction last year alone\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The reference is not a supplied consensus, guidance figure, or official target, so it has no rung; rung 3 is the 2-to-below-4 bucket, not a reference. The forecast remains one qualifying state beyond New York, which is rung 2: the proposal pipeline and political pressure raise the chance of further action, but the supplied items do not report another qualifying enactment, and proposals, local actions, or grid constraints alone do not count. The evidence does not imply the two states needed to cross into rung 3.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.', 'user_full': 'Thesis v18 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"The number and scope of U.S. states enacting new data-center construction moratoriums or material restrictions (beyond New York\\'s one-year moratorium) in calendar 2026.\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How many U.S. states other than New York enact new data-center construction moratoriums or material restrictions during calendar 2026, as verified by state legislative records, enacted statutes and official governor announcements available by January 31,