{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json,pathlib,collections; p=pathlib.Path(\\\"poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20\\\");\nfor d, cs in [(\\\"full\\\",json.loads((p/\\\"fullcases.json\\\").read_text())),(\\\"probe\\\",json.loads((p/\\\"cases.json\\\").read_text()))]:\n if d==\\\"full\\\": cs=[c for c in cs if c[\\\"hidden\\\"]>=1]\n sc=json.loads((p/(\\\"marks-\\\"+d+\\\".json\\\")).read_text()); arms=sorted({x[\\\"arm\\\"] for x in sc.values() if x[\\\"kind\\\"]\"'!=\"plant\"}); actual=collections.Counter((x[\"arm\"],x[\"case\"],x[\"repeat\"]) for x in sc.values() if x[\"kind\"]!=\"plant\"); expected={(a,c[\"case\"],r) for a in arms for c in cs for r in (1,2,3)}; assert set(actual)==expected and set(actual.values())=={1}; holds=collections.defaultdict(list); sup=collections.Counter((x[\"arm\"],x[\"case\"]) for x in sc.values() if x[\"kind\"]!=\"plant\" and x[\"marks\"][\"answer\"]==\"supported\");\n for a in arms:\n  for c in cs:\n   if sup[a,c[\"case\"]]>=2: holds[(a,c[\"role\"])].append(c[\"case\"])\n print(d,len(expected),[(a,len(holds[a,\"target\"]),len(holds[a,\"control\"])) for a in arms],\"plants\",[x[\"marks\"][\"answer\"] for x in sc.values() if x[\"kind\"]==\"plant\"])\nfor cid in (\"C073\",\"C100\",\"C108\",\"C119\",\"C043\",\"C116\",\"C123\",\"C035\",\"C068\",\"C082\"):\n print(cid)\n for reader in (\"claude\",\"codex\"):\n  for i in (2,1,0):\n   f=p/\"deck-full\"/(\"round-\"+str(i))/(cid+\"-\"+reader+\".json\")\n   if f.exists():\n    m=json.loads(f.read_text()); print(reader,i,m); break'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import json,pathlib,collections; p=pathlib.Path(\\\"poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20\\\");\nfor d, cs in [(\\\"full\\\",json.loads((p/\\\"fullcases.json\\\").read_text())),(\\\"probe\\\",json.loads((p/\\\"cases.json\\\").read_text()))]:\n if d==\\\"full\\\": cs=[c for c in cs if c[\\\"hidden\\\"]>=1]\n sc=json.loads((p/(\\\"marks-\\\"+d+\\\".json\\\")).read_text()); arms=sorted({x[\\\"arm\\\"] for x in sc.values() if x[\\\"kind\\\"]\"'!=\"plant\"}); actual=collections.Counter((x[\"arm\"],x[\"case\"],x[\"repeat\"]) for x in sc.values() if x[\"kind\"]!=\"plant\"); expected={(a,c[\"case\"],r) for a in arms for c in cs for r in (1,2,3)}; assert set(actual)==expected and set(actual.values())=={1}; holds=collections.defaultdict(list); sup=collections.Counter((x[\"arm\"],x[\"case\"]) for x in sc.values() if x[\"kind\"]!=\"plant\" and x[\"marks\"][\"answer\"]==\"supported\");\n for a in arms:\n  for c in cs:\n   if sup[a,c[\"case\"]]>=2: holds[(a,c[\"role\"])].append(c[\"case\"])\n print(d,len(expected),[(a,len(holds[a,\"target\"]),len(holds[a,\"control\"])) for a in arms],\"plants\",[x[\"marks\"][\"answer\"] for x in sc.values() if x[\"kind\"]==\"plant\"])\nfor cid in (\"C073\",\"C100\",\"C108\",\"C119\",\"C043\",\"C116\",\"C123\",\"C035\",\"C068\",\"C082\"):\n print(cid)\n for reader in (\"claude\",\"codex\"):\n  for i in (2,1,0):\n   f=p/\"deck-full\"/(\"round-\"+str(i))/(cid+\"-\"+reader+\".json\")\n   if f.exists():\n    m=json.loads(f.read_text()); print(reader,i,m); break'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"full 126 [('v7-fullnoprev', 4, 8), ('v7-fullprev', 0, 8)] plants ['unsupported', 'unsupported', 'unsupported']\nprobe 432 [('J2a', 2, 22), ('J2b', 4, 23), ('v7', 1, 21)] plants ['unsupported', 'unsupported', 'unsupported']\nC073\nclaude 0 {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'unsupported', 'why': \"Outstanding commitments of ~$25B bear on NVIDIA's cash and credit risk, and no earlier question tracks them. But $18B runs off by fiscal year-end, leaving ~$7B plus partly-funded new deals, which points to rung 1.\"}\ncodex 0 {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'unsupported', 'why': 'Equity commitments are financially material and distinct from credit support. Of the $25 billion outstanding in July, $18 billion was committed for the fiscal-year remainder; disclosed subsequent deals do not establish enough outstanding replenishment to favor at least $25 billion.'}\nC100\nclaude 1 {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'unsupported', 'why': 'The answer rests on ignoring the $18B rest-of-year draw. That draw leaves about $7B of the $25B, which is rung 1. Getting back above $10B depends on unconfirmed or quickly-funded new deals, so the $20–25B estimate contradicts the material.'}\ncodex 1 {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'unsupported', 'why': 'Equity commitments are material and distinct from earlier questions. The $18 billion earmarked for the rest of FY2027 suggests substantial drawdown of commitments; the materials do not establish enough new, outstanding commitments to justify the stated $20–25 billion estimate.'}\nC108\nclaude 2 {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'supported', 'why': 'Changing to supported. The $25–30B estimate ignores runoff, but after funding about $18B, roughly $7B remains. New post-July deals (MediaTek, Poolside, SB Energy, Lancium, Thinking Machines, a possible Anthropic IPO) plausibly keep the balance at $10–30B, favouring rung 2.'}\ncodex 2 {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'supported', 'why': 'The $25 billion commitment balance is material and distinct from earlier questions. It anchors the $10–30 billion range; the material does not establish that the $18 billion is part of that balance or will be funded before filing.'}\nC119\nclaude 2 {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'unsupported', 'why': \"The $18B is due this fiscal year, leaving about $7B already committed for later years. Post-quarter deals (Lancium, SB Energy, Thinking Machines, Perplexity, possible $10B Anthropic) and NVIDIA's deal pace make clearing $10B more likely, favouring rung 2.\"}\ncodex 2 {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'supported', 'why': 'The $25 billion commitment balance is material and distinct from earlier questions. The $18 billion earmarked for the remaining fiscal year supports a substantial drawdown; later deals and negotiations could offset it, but their timing and outstanding amounts are uncertain.'}\nC043\nclaude 0 {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'unsupported', 'why': \"A ~$20B new CPU line can move revenue, so it is worth tracking. But guidance says 'nearly $20 billion', which sits just below rung 2's floor, and the answer ignores the >$5B Grace run-rate that a 'total' count would include.\"}\ncodex 0 {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'unsupported', 'why': 'CPU revenue at this scale is material and distinct from total company revenue. Management’s underlying reference is nearly $20 billion, below rung 2’s floor; rounded reports and production progress do not establish that the threshold will be reached.'}\nC116\nclaude 1 {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'supported', 'why': \"Management called ~$20B 'total CPU' revenue, so adding Grace's >$5B risks double counting; rung 1 lacks basis. 'Nearly' pulls toward rung 3, but standalone-only scope pulls upward. Rung 2 is defensible.\"}\ncodex 1 {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'supported', 'why': 'CPU revenue is material and distinct from total company revenue. The roughly $20 billion FY2027 forecast supports rung 2; Grace’s trailing revenue does not establish how much additional FY2027 revenue should be added or whether the total exceeds $25 billion.'}\nC123\nclaude 2 {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'unsupported', 'why': \"Keep unsupported: NVIDIA's own figure is 'nearly $20B in total CPU revenue,' reaffirmed 26 Aug as '~$20B total server CPUs', pointing to rung 2. Rung 1 needs full Vera plus undiminished Grace, and still barely clears.\"}\ncodex 2 {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'unsupported', 'why': 'CPU revenue is material and distinct from company-wide revenue. NVIDIA’s nearly $20 billion forecast and approximately $20 billion total server-CPU demand favor a lower rung; carrying Grace’s trailing revenue forward does not establish that the combined total clears $25 billion.'}\nC035\nclaude 0 {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'unsupported', 'why': 'Distinct, large cash-commitment bet. The answer double-counts: the $18B for the rest of FY2027 is likely part of the $25B outstanding, not extra. Funding it leaves about $7B plus new deals, pointing to rung 1.'}\ncodex 0 {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'unsupported', 'why': 'The obligations are material and distinct from credit support. The $18 billion due during FY2027 is part of the $25 billion commitment balance, not an addition; funding it would reduce that balance. Subsequent activity does not justify exceeding $35 billion.'}\nC068\nclaude 0 {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'unsupported', 'why': \"Equity commitments (~$25B) are material cash risk and distinct from the credit-support and purchase-commitment cards. The $18B is likely funded out of the $25B, not added to it; with Huang's no-repeat remark, rung 1 is better supported.\"}\ncodex 0 {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'unsupported', 'why': 'Equity commitments are financially material and distinct from earlier credit-support questions. The $18 billion due during FY2027 likely overlaps the $25 billion outstanding; funding reduces the year-end balance. The cited new investments do not justify lifting it above $35 billion.'}\nC082\nclaude 0 {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'supported', 'why': 'A $25B equity-commitment balance is material cash exposure, separate from guarantees and capacity obligations. With $18B due to be funded this fiscal year, about $28B of net new commitments would be needed to reach $35B; announced deals fall well short.'}\ncodex 0 {'worth': 'worth', 'answer': 'supported', 'why': 'Equity commitments are material and distinct from the earlier credit-support and purchase-obligation questions. The $25 billion outstanding balance supports the below-$35 billion forecast; planned funding and subsequent deals do not establish a higher year-end balance.'}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sys,json,collections; sys.path.insert(0,\\\"docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid\\\"); import judge_probe as p; e,q=p.gold_entries(); e={d[\\\"question_id\\\"]:d for d in e}; print(\\\"q17 gold\\\",e[\\\"q17\\\"][\\\"implied_outcome\\\"]); cs=json.loads((p.OUT/\\\"goldcases.json\\\").read_text());\nfor arm in (\\\"v7\\\",\\\"v7-noprev\\\",\\\"J2a\\\",\\\"J2b\\\"):\n p.goldscore(arm); tally=collections.Counter();\n for c in cs:\n  for r in (1,2,3):\n   a=json.loads((p.OUT/\\\"goldprobe\\\"/arm/(\\\"{}-{}.json\\\".format(c[\\\"case\\\"],r))).read_text()); rung=a[\\\"answer\\\"][\\\"rung\\\"]; y=1<=rung<=len(c[\\\"rungs\\\"]) and p.gold_agrees(e[c[\\\"qid\\\"]],q[c[\\\"qid\\\"]],c[\\\"rungs\\\"],rung); assert y==a[\\\"agrees\\\"]; tally[r]+=y\n print(\\\"recomputed\\\",arm,dict(tally))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'import sys,json,collections; sys.path.insert(0,\\\"docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid\\\"); import judge_probe as p; e,q=p.gold_entries(); e={d[\\\"question_id\\\"]:d for d in e}; print(\\\"q17 gold\\\",e[\\\"q17\\\"][\\\"implied_outcome\\\"]); cs=json.loads((p.OUT/\\\"goldcases.json\\\").read_text());\nfor arm in (\\\"v7\\\",\\\"v7-noprev\\\",\\\"J2a\\\",\\\"J2b\\\"):\n p.goldscore(arm); tally=collections.Counter();\n for c in cs:\n  for r in (1,2,3):\n   a=json.loads((p.OUT/\\\"goldprobe\\\"/arm/(\\\"{}-{}.json\\\".format(c[\\\"case\\\"],r))).read_text()); rung=a[\\\"answer\\\"][\\\"rung\\\"]; y=1<=rung<=len(c[\\\"rungs\\\"]) and p.gold_agrees(e[c[\\\"qid\\\"]],q[c[\\\"qid\\\"]],c[\\\"rungs\\\"],rung); assert y==a[\\\"agrees\\\"]; tally[r]+=y\n print(\\\"recomputed\\\",arm,dict(tally))'\"","aggregated_output":"q17 gold {'text': \"Beijing has refused H200 purchases so far (i304); if The Information's reported plan to allow fewer than 200,000 H200s for Alibaba, ByteDance and DeepSeek proceeds (i270), shipments land late in the year, a few billion dollars of a roughly $50bn market (i227); Huawei holds about half (i227), Hygon and Cambricon are being evaluated (i187), and buyers plan to move to 46% domestic (i183); an outside full-year estimate may also count smuggled hardware, $2.5bn in one case (i307). I bet 1-8%.\", 'low': 1, 'high': 8, 'yes_no': None, 'items': ['i304', 'i270', 'i227', 'i187', 'i183', 'i307']}\nv7: agreement per repeat {1: 45, 2: 46, 3: 44} of 51; counting refused as not agreeing {1: 45, 2: 46, 3: 42}; refused answers 3 of 153\nrecomputed v7 {1: 45, 2: 46, 3: 44}\nv7-noprev: agreement per repeat {1: 43, 2: 45, 3: 45} of 51; counting refused as not agreeing {1: 42, 2: 45, 3: 44}; refused answers 2 of 153\nrecomputed v7-noprev {1: 43, 2: 45, 3: 45}\nJ2a: agreement per repeat {1: 44, 2: 44, 3: 45} of 51; counting refused as not agreeing {1: 43, 2: 44, 3: 45}; refused answers 1 of 153\nrecomputed J2a {1: 44, 2: 44, 3: 45}\nJ2b: agreement per repeat {1: 44, 2: 45, 3: 45} of 51; counting refused as not agreeing {1: 44, 2: 44, 3: 44}; refused answers 2 of 153\nrecomputed J2b {1: 44, 2: 45, 3: 45}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/deck-full/C100.txt | sed -n '1,15p;81,117p'; nl -ba poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/deck-full/C108.txt | sed -n '1,15p'; rg -n 'items_path|items.sqlite|ITEMS' docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py docs/research/2026-11-19-merge-council/merge_test.py docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/stage0/build_cards.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/deck-full/C100.txt | sed -n '1,15p;81,117p'; nl -ba poc/data/hybrid-2026-11-20/deck-full/C108.txt | sed -n '1,15p'; rg -n 'items_path|items.sqlite|ITEMS' docs/research/2026-11-20-hybrid/judge_probe.py docs/research/2026-11-19-merge-council/merge_test.py docs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/stage0/build_cards.py\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tQuestion: At NVIDIA’s FY2027 annual filing, what amount of equity investment commitments will NVIDIA report as outstanding or committed?\n     2\tOpened: 2026-08-12\n     3\tDue: 2027-02-28\n     4\tPossible answers (numbered as the reasons cite them):\n     5\t1. NVIDIA reports less than $10 billion of outstanding or committed equity investment commitments.\n     6\t2. NVIDIA reports at least $10 billion but less than $30 billion.\n     7\t3. NVIDIA reports at least $30 billion but less than $50 billion.\n     8\t4. NVIDIA reports at least $50 billion but less than $70 billion.\n     9\t5. NVIDIA reports at least $70 billion.\n    10\t\n    11\tLatest answer at the cutoff: NVIDIA reports at least $10 billion but less than $30 billion.\n    12\tReason given: Reference: The latest company-reported balance, $25 billion of equity investment commitments as of July 26, 2026, is the reference and sits in rung 2. No separate market consensus for the FY2027 ending balance is supplied. Implied outcome: Approximately $20–25 billion outstanding or committed at the annual filing: the $25 billion reported July balance anchors the estimate, while the later $18 billion remainder-of-FY commitment is not shown to be additive or to remain outstanding at year-end. Reasons: The latest company-reported balance is $25 billion, in rung 2. NVIDIA later said $18 billion was committed for the remainder of FY2027, but the evidence does not establish that this is additive to the July balance or that it will remain outstanding at the annual filing; it is a remainder-of-year commitment, not an ending balance. The implied ending amount is therefore around $20–25 billion, below the $30 billion rung-3 boundary. NVIDIA’s statement that several very large FY2027 investments would not recur next year also argues against projecting another comparable step-up.\n    13\t\n    14\tMaterial that answer was given (96 items, by day):\n    15\t1. (third_party_view; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker Yahoo Finance; original source Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya; published 2026-08-12) Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya said NVIDIA has committed $70 billion in equity investments in ecosystem partners; the period was not specified.\n    81\t34. (party_statement; publisher NVIDIA Corporation; speaker NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA said its equity investments focus on AI model makers, infrastructure financiers, and other private companies, subject to contingencies.\n    82\t   Quote: Our equity investments are focused on AI model makers, infrastructure financiers, and other private companies, subject to certain contingencies.\n    83\t35. (fact; publisher NVIDIA Corporation; speaker NVIDIA Corporation; original source NVIDIA Corporation 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA reported $99 billion of equity investments and $25 billion of equity investment commitments as of July 26, 2026.\n    84\t   Quote: We have made, and may continue to make, investments and commitments in our ecosystem to enhance our growth opportunities, cultivate our ecosystem, and strengthen our competitive position. These include equity investments of $99 billion and equity investment commitments of $25 billion as of July 26, 2026.\n    85\t36. (party_statement; publisher cnbc.com; speaker Jensen Huang; original source Jensen Huang 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) Jensen Huang said NVIDIA had several very large AI-lab investments in FY2027 and did not expect those investments to recur next year.\n    86\t   Quote: furthermore, this year we have several very large investments. And those large investments in AI labs are not going to repeat.\n    87\t37. (party_statement; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker finance.yahoo.com; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said NVIDIA had invested or committed nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs.\n    88\t   Quote: Nvidia has invested or committed nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs, Kress said.\n    89\t38. (rumour; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker finance.yahoo.com; original source finance.yahoo.com, reporting unnamed sources; published 2026-08-26) Unnamed sources reported on 2026-08-26 that NVIDIA was in advanced talks to invest in Perplexity at a valuation above US$30 billion; the investment amount and stake size were not stated.\n    90\t   Quote: Nvidia (NasdaqGS: NVDA) is in advanced talks to invest in AI startup Perplexity at a valuation above US$30b, according to people familiar with the matter.\n    91\t39. (third_party_view; publisher gurufocus.com; speaker gurufocus.com; original source Bank of America; published 2026-08-26) Bank of America estimated NVIDIA’s committed capital to date at about $300 billion, including about $70 billion of direct equity investments and $230 billion of guarantees or backstops.\n    92\t   Quote: The firm estimates roughly $300 billion of committed capital to date, including about $70 billion of direct equity investments and $230 billion of guarantees or backstops.\n    93\t40. (party_statement; publisher marketbeat.com; speaker MarketBeat; original source Colette Kress, NVIDIA CFO; published 2026-08-26) Colette Kress said NVIDIA had invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI laboratories.\n    94\t   Quote: Kress said NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI laboratories\n    95\t41. (third_party_view; publisher barchart.com; speaker barchart.com author; original source barchart.com author; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA was reported to be in talks about taking a stake in Perplexity AI at a valuation of $30 billion, up from $20 billion last year.\n    96\t   Quote: And now it's in talks with Perplexity AI as it considers taking a stake that would value the AI startup at $30 billion, an increase from $20 billion last year.\n    97\t42. (third_party_view; publisher barchart.com; speaker barchart.com author; original source Perplexity AI; published 2026-08-26) Perplexity AI's annualized revenue increased in 2026 from $250 million to more than $750 million.\n    98\t   Quote: Perplexity, which is an AI-powered platform that searches the internet and provides answers with numbered source citations, has seen its annualized revenue increase this year from $250 million to more than $750 million.\n    99\t43. (party_statement; publisher taekim.substack.com; speaker NVIDIA, quoted by taekim.substack.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA said it expected Anthropic and OpenAI to become the largest technology companies in history and said it expected excellent returns and limited risk from its investments in them.\n   100\t   Quote: “We expect them to become the largest technology companies in history. We believe these investments, measured against the strength of their demand, the business they create for us, the ecosystem they build on Nvidia’s platform, and the equity returns on our invested capital will be excellent, and our risk is limited. The Nvidia Compute platform is fungible and durable and can be redeployed to supp\n   101\t44. (fact; publisher fortune.com; speaker Fortune; original source NVIDIA; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA has publicized investments totaling nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs.\n   102\t   Quote: Nvidia has also publicized investments of nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs\n   103\t45. (party_statement; publisher fortune.com; speaker Fortune, reporting Colette Kress's remarks; original source Colette Kress 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) CFO Colette Kress characterized NVIDIA's partner investments as low-risk and high-reward.\n   104\t   Quote: Kress said the investments in partners were low-risk, high-reward for Nvidia.\n   105\t46. (party_statement; publisher fortune.com; speaker Colette Kress; original source Colette Kress 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) Colette Kress said NVIDIA expected excellent returns and limited risk from its partner investments, considering their demand, business generated, ecosystem value, and equity returns.\n   106\t   Quote: “We believe these investments, measured against the strength of their demand, the business they create for us, the ecosystem they build on Nvidia’s platform, and the equity returns on our invested capital will be excellent, and our risk is limited,” said Kress.\n   107\t47. (party_statement; publisher businessinsider.com; speaker Business Insider, reporting NVIDIA's statement; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-26) NVIDIA said it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the remainder of FY2027, in addition to $47.9 billion in existing private-company holdings.\n   108\t   Quote: The company said Wednesday that it has $18 billion committed to equity investments for the rest of its fiscal year, on top of $47.9 billion it already holds in private companies.\n   109\t48. (party_statement; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress, quoted by Yahoo Finance; original source Colette Kress 2026-08-26; published 2026-08-27) NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said the company expects its investments’ returns to be excellent and considers its risk limited.\n   110\t   Quote: “We believe these investments, measured against the strength of their demand, the business they create for us, the ecosystem they build on Nvidia's platform, and the equity returns on our invested capital will be excellent, and our risk is limited,” Kress said on the call.\n   111\t49. (party_statement; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA; published 2026-08-27) NVIDIA has earmarked $18 billion for equity investments through the rest of its fiscal year.\n   112\t   Quote: Nvidia has also earmarked $18 billion for equity investments through the rest of this fiscal year, part of a shift CNBC has described as Nvidia's moat moving from chips to capital .\n   113\t50. (fact; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA; published 2026-08-28) NVIDIA's future commitments for cloud services, leases, equity investments and capital expenditures totaled $143 billion.\n   114\t   Quote: while future commitments for cloud services, leases, equity investments and capital expenditures reached $143 billion.\n   115\t51. (fact; publisher gurufocus.com; speaker gurufocus.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA; published 2026-08-28) Nvidia reported $99 billion in equity investments and $25 billion in equity investment commitments.\n   116\t   Quote: The company also held $99 billion in equity investments and another $25 billion in equity investment commitments .\n   117\t52. (fact; publisher fortune.com; speaker Fortune, reporting NVIDIA’s 10-Q; original source NVIDIA; published 2026-08-28) NVIDIA disclosed $25 billion in equity investments in its Q2 FY2027 10-Q.\n     1\tQuestion: At NVIDIA’s FY2027 annual filing, what amount of equity investment commitments will NVIDIA report as outstanding or committed?\n     2\tOpened: 2026-08-12\n     3\tDue: 2027-02-28\n     4\tPossible answers (numbered as the reasons cite them):\n     5\t1. NVIDIA reports less than $10 billion of outstanding or committed equity investment commitments.\n     6\t2. NVIDIA reports at least $10 billion but less than $30 billion.\n     7\t3. NVIDIA reports at least $30 billion but less than $50 billion.\n     8\t4. NVIDIA reports at least $50 billion but less than $70 billion.\n     9\t5. NVIDIA reports at least $70 billion.\n    10\t\n    11\tLatest answer at the cutoff: NVIDIA reports at least $10 billion but less than $30 billion.\n    12\tReason given: Reference: The latest company-reported commitment balance is $25 billion as of July 26, 2026, placing the reference in rung 2 ($10 billion to less than $30 billion). No separate market consensus is supplied. Implied outcome: Approximately $25–30 billion outstanding or committed at the FY2027 annual filing: the $25 billion reported balance is the starting point, while the $18 billion committed for the rest of FY2027 is not added dollar-for-dollar because it may be funded before the filing. Reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s reported $25 billion of equity investment commitments as of July 26, 2026, which sits in rung 2; no separate market consensus is supplied. The $18 billion committed for the rest of FY2027 is meaningful forward activity, but it is not evidence that $18 billion will remain outstanding at the annual filing: commitments can be funded before then. The more than $42 billion spent on equity stakes in the first half and management’s statement that this year’s very large AI-lab investments will not recur argue against carrying the exceptional pace forward as a growing year-end commitment balance. The evidence therefore points to roughly $25–30 billion outstanding at filing, still below the $30 billion rung-3 boundary. The BofA estimate of $70 billion refers to equity investments, not the outstanding-commitments figure asked here.\n    13\t\n    14\tMaterial that answer was given (96 items, by day):\n    15\t1. (third_party_view; publisher finance.yahoo.com; speaker Yahoo Finance; original source Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya; published 2026-08-12) Bank of America analyst Vivek Arya said NVIDIA has committed $70 billion in equity investments in ecosystem partners; the period was not specified.\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/stage0/build_cards.py:106:def library(views_path, archive_path, items_path):\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/stage0/build_cards.py:107:    conn, archive, items = ro(views_path), ro(archive_path), ro(items_path)\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/stage0/build_cards.py:170:def main(views_path, archive_path, items_path, out_path):\ndocs/research/2026-11-17-product-comparison/stage0/build_cards.py:171:    lib = library(views_path, archive_path, items_path)\ndocs/research/2026-11-19-merge-council/merge_test.py:26:ITEMS = ROOT / 'poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/items-comparison.sqlite'\ndocs/research/2026-11-19-merge-council/merge_test.py:115:    item = runner.selected_items(str(ITEMS), dict(manifest, item_ids=[input_id], item_limit=1))[0]\ndocs/research/2026-11-19-merge-council/merge_test.py:153:            outcome = runner.run(str(ITEMS), folder / 'trace.sqlite', dict(manifest, item_ids=[entry['input_id']], item_limit=1),\ndocs/research/2026-11-19-merge-council/merge_test.py:489:    conn, archive, items = build_cards.ro(views_path), build_cards.ro(archive_path), build_cards.ro(ITEMS)\ndocs/research/2026-11-19-merge-council/merge_test.py:523:        old_lib = build_cards.library(RUN / draw / 'prod/views.sqlite', RUN / draw / 'prod/archive.sqlite', ITEMS)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
